WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:06.200
I was going to come in today and tell you
that a Democrat has a chance to defeat a Republican

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.080
in the state of Montana.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:13.249
But if I told you that, it wouldn't be accurate,
because it's not that a Democrat has a chance

00:00:13.249 --> 00:00:15.440
to defeat a Republican in Montana.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.759
The truth is that a Democrat is leading by
seven against a Republican in Montana.

00:00:20.759 --> 00:00:23.020
This is a really, really big deal.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:27.730
We'll talk about Montana, then we'll apply
it a little bit broadly in 2016.

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:35.910
Donald Trump won Montana 56 to 36 over Hillary
Clinton 20 point win in that state.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:44.260
In 2014, Republican Steve Daines won his Senate
race over Amanda Curtis, also by a big margin

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:48.060
by 18, 58 to 40.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:56.140
But incumbent Republican Senator Steve Daines,
who won in 2014, is currently down 7.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:02.650
To Steve Bullock in the very red state of
Montana, Steve Bullock is the current governor

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:04.379
of Montana.

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:09.990
According to the new Montana State University
poll released yesterday, he is ahead by seven

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.340
against the incumbent Republican senator.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:17.290
Now, if the name Steve Bullock sounds familiar,
it should not just because he's the current

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:21.040
governor, but he also was not not particularly
successful.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:27.080
But he was in the 2020 Democratic primary
race for a little while and even was in at

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:28.080
least one debate.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.290
I don't remember now exactly how many debates,
but he was at least one debate.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:37.560
I think we've been talking about the Senate
balance in 2020.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:44.950
And Democrats need to have a net gain of three
seats plus the presidency or a net gain of

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:45.950
four seats.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:49.500
If Trump gets reelected to take control of
the Senate.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.500
Now, why?

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:51.500
What does that mean?

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.410
Why does that matter whether Trump wins or
loses?

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:57.520
If you have a Senate that's 50/50, the tiebreaker
is the vise president.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.760
Of course, if the tiebreaker is Mike Pence,
that doesn't go Democrats way.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:06.340
So if Trump were to get reelected, Democrats
would need to get to 51 seats.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.319
If Biden wins, they only need to get to 50.

00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:13.190
So we're assuming and I believe that it's
a fair assumption that Democrats are going

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:16.519
to lose the Doug Jones seat in Alabama.

00:02:16.519 --> 00:02:18.180
That's the special election seat.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.650
It used to be Jeff Sessions, Senate seat.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:25.690
Jeff Sessions was made Donald Trump's beleaguered
attorney general.

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:28.849
So that seat was up for special election.

00:02:28.849 --> 00:02:37.300
Doug Jones won against the Republican nominee
who was serial accused sex assaulter Roy Moore.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:43.620
Now, Jeff Sessions is trying to win his old
seat back and he's winning very, very handily

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.099
in the polling in Alabama.

00:02:45.099 --> 00:02:48.450
So there's every indication Democrats are
going to lose that seat in Alabama.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:51.890
So to net three, you've got to find four wins.

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:56.249
To net four, you've got to find five wins
because you're gonna lose that one seat.

00:02:56.249 --> 00:02:57.569
So what are we looking at?

00:02:57.569 --> 00:03:01.849
Arizona we've talked about Arizona before,
Mark Kelly, against the incumbent Republican,

00:03:01.849 --> 00:03:03.150
Martha McSally.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:07.430
She is in the seat previously held by John
McCain, the late John McCain.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:08.709
She's not won any election.

00:03:08.709 --> 00:03:11.249
She was appointed there, Maine.

00:03:11.249 --> 00:03:17.749
Sarah Gideon is doing well against longtime
incumbent Senator Susan Collins, a Republican

00:03:17.749 --> 00:03:22.760
who's often disappointed with Trump, but eventually
sides with him in Colorado.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:26.519
Cory Gardner is not doing particularly well
in North Carolina.

00:03:26.519 --> 00:03:29.459
Thom Tillis could lose, which would be great.

00:03:29.459 --> 00:03:33.430
But now, with the addition of Montana, this
adds a new element.

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:37.999
Now, a few caveats on the seven point lead
that Steve Bullock has in Montana.

00:03:37.999 --> 00:03:40.790
Number one, it's forty six to thirty nine.

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:45.519
So it's a seven point lead, but forty six
plus thirty nine is only eighty five.

00:03:45.519 --> 00:03:48.200
That leaves fifteen up in the air.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.659
And sometimes that breaks for the incumbent.

00:03:50.659 --> 00:03:53.470
So that's one caveat.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:58.609
One of the reasons that Bullock is doing well
in Montana is that as the current governor,

00:03:58.609 --> 00:04:01.099
as I told you, he's done really well with
Corona virus.

00:04:01.099 --> 00:04:06.989
He has a 70 percent approval on his handling
of Corona virus, only a 48 percent approval

00:04:06.989 --> 00:04:09.339
for the incumbent Senator Steve Daines.

00:04:09.339 --> 00:04:13.579
In terms of his handling of Corona virus,
of course, governors and senators have different

00:04:13.579 --> 00:04:16.489
roles in the context of Corona virus.

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:22.770
2020 could quickly become Senate Majority
Leader Mitch McConnell's worst nightmare if

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:25.539
some of these races go the Democrats way.

00:04:25.539 --> 00:04:27.210
But it's up to us to make it so.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:31.039
We need to get out and vote and not assume
any single race in any state.

00:04:31.039 --> 00:04:35.330
Now, I have other outrageous news for Montana
as well.

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:39.020
There is a hypothetical presidential polling
being done.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:43.110
I guess at this point it's not really hypothetical
in the sense that Joe Biden's the only candidate

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:44.110
left.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:49.919
Trump is only ahead by five in the presidential
polling against Joe Biden in Montana.

00:04:49.919 --> 00:04:55.250
Remember, in 2016, Trump beat Hillary by 20
in Montana.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:59.500
Now, admittedly, libertarian Gary Johnson
got 5.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:01.490
Points in Montana in 2016.

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:07.460
So maybe that these numbers need to be adjusted
for that, but this is a big swing.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:08.780
Call it a ten point swing.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:10.850
Call it a 15 point swing.

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:18.569
If indeed Donald Trump only wins Montana by
five, it would be a very bad November for

00:05:18.569 --> 00:05:19.919
Donald Trump.

00:05:19.919 --> 00:05:23.379
Does it pass the sniff test that Trump is
only up five in Montana?

00:05:23.379 --> 00:05:24.379
I don't know.

00:05:24.379 --> 00:05:25.470
I honestly don't know.

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:26.470
These are early numbers.

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:27.990
Anyway, we shouldn't be assuming anything.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:29.440
There's much to work for.

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:34.150
But the Senate, which a year ago I would look
two years ago, I was saying Democrats don't

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:37.849
really have a chance in November of twenty
twenty for the Senate.

00:05:37.849 --> 00:05:42.270
One year ago I was saying it's possible, but
it's a big uphill battle.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:45.460
Six months ago, I was saying it's possible.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:47.770
Now, this is a real possibility.

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:48.890
I don't I don't think it's likely.

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:49.890
I think.

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:53.460
I still think if I had a bet, I would say
you got to give me odds to bet on Democrats

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:55.219
in taking the Senate.

00:05:55.219 --> 00:06:00.050
But there is now a very clear path and it
is not a wholly unrealistic path.

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:03.569
Democrats would need a couple of these races
to go against the incumbent in the end.

00:06:03.569 --> 00:06:07.639
That can be tough, but a lot of good races
to get involved in and really great numbers

00:06:07.639 --> 00:06:08.639
out of Montana.

00:06:08.639 --> 00:06:08.643
We'll keep following it.

